 **`focus()` on a window that lives on another desktop switches to that desktop first**, so focus never lands on a window the user cannot see. `HOOKS.DESKTOP_SWITCHED` fires before the window's own `WINDOW_FOCUSED`. `open()` never does this: a window opened onto an inactive desktop (session restore, a lazily loaded native window) joins it without taking focus, and the active desktop stays where it is.
 
+<a id="keep-on-top"></a>
+#### Keep on top — Experimental
+
+A window kept on top stays above every window that is not, even while another window has focus or a new one opens. The user switches it with the pin button in the title bar (`core/always-on-top`, just before minimize); code switches it with `setAlwaysOnTop()` and reads it off the window:
+
+```javascript
+const { windowManager } = wp.os;
+windowManager.setAlwaysOnTop( 'my-plugin-timer', true );
+windowManager.getById( 'my-plugin-timer' ).alwaysOnTop; // true
+```
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **Focus** | Unchanged. Focusing another window gives it focus, and it still paints under the kept-on-top one. `getFocused()` keeps returning the focused window, not the topmost painted one. |
+| **Several at once** | Kept-on-top windows stack among themselves in focus order, above everything else. |
+| **Turning it off** | The window goes back to its place in focus order, which may be under windows focused since. |
+| **Child windows** | A child of a kept-on-top owner paints in the same layer, so it never ends up under the window it blocks. |
+| **Fullscreen** | A fullscreen window still covers everything, kept-on-top windows included. |
+| **Session** | Saved with the window (`alwaysOnTop: true`) and restored on reload. |
+
+`setAlwaysOnTop()` is a no-op for an unknown id or when the value does not change. A change fires [`os-window-always-on-top-changed`](#os-window-always-on-top-changed--experimental) and the `os.window.always-on-top-changed` action. To open a window already kept on top, pass `alwaysOnTop: true` in the `open()` config.
+
 #### Child windows — Stable
 
 A **child window** is a real window — own chrome, drag, resize, minimize, taskbar entry — with one rule layered on top: **its owner can never sit above it.** Clicking the owner hands focus to the child and shakes it rather than raising the owner.
